{"chunk_id": "6313827cc8e9", "text": "This section transitions from the Pandavas\u2019 recovery of immense wealth to events in Hastinapura as preparations for Yudhishthira\u2019s horse sacrifice proceed. Krishna returns from Dwaraka with many Vrishni heroes to visit the Kuru capital, partly to comfort the royal women after the devastating losses of the war. During their stay, Uttara gives birth to Parikshit, the posthumous son of Abhimanyu. The child, however, is born lifeless, having been struck in the womb by Ashwatthaman\u2019s Brahma-weapon. The city\u2019s initial celebration turns instantly to grief. Kunti, Draupadi, Subhadra, and the other women plead with Krishna to fulfill his earlier promise to restore the child to life, since the survival of the Pandava line\u2014and the ancestral rites of their family\u2014depend on him. The scene underscores the fragility of dynasties after war and Krishna\u2019s role as divine protector of the Kuru lineage.\nKrishna returns to the Kuru capital; Parikshit is born lifeless from Ashwatthama\u2019s Brahma-weapon, and Kunti begs Krishna to revive the Pandava heir.\nThemes: divine intervention, dynastic survival, grief after war, Krishna\u2019s protection, birth of Parikshit", "vector": [0.0010316645493730903, -0.028988583013415337, -0.006723665166646242, -0.0014633659739047289, -0.0128422686830163, 0.01758078671991825, -0.016547422856092453, -0.0027210789266973734, -0.006886828225106001, -0.02257084846496582, 0.01729525253176689, 0.001173582160845399, 0.0019494550069794059, 0.00907592847943306, -0.007668649777770042, -0.010823129676282406, 0.04315654933452606, -0.016751376911997795, 0.02975000999867916, -0.015799593180418015, 0.0027754665352404118, -0.0061865877360105515, 0.0019528542179614305, 0.0206264927983284, -0.011088268831372261, 0.027329761534929276, 0.01128542423248291, -0.014956586062908173, 0.015146942809224129, -0.018056677654385567, -0.024488011375069618, -0.006376944482326508, -0.0049730646423995495, -0.016751